### What is the API Gateway pattern?

The API Gateway pattern puts one entry point in front of all services. It handles routing, authentication, rate limiting, and request aggregation. Clients never talk to services directly.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator routes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
            .route("payments", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                .filters(f -> f.circuitBreaker(c -> c.setName("paymentCB")))
                .uri("lb://payment-service"))
            .route("accounts", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                .uri("lb://account-service"))
            .build();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected routing:
/api/v1/payments/PAY-001 -> payment-service (load balanced)
/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001 -> account-service (load balanced)
```

### What is the Service Discovery pattern?

Services register under a logical name and callers resolve it. This solves the problem that instances come and go with scaling and deploys.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.FeignClient;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@FeignClient(name = "account-service")  // resolved via service registry
public interface AccountClient {

    @PostMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/debit")
    void debit(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody DebitRequest request);
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Feign resolves "account-service" via Eureka -> picks instance 192.168.1.10:8081
If that instance goes down, next call picks 192.168.1.11:8081 automatically.
```

### What is the Circuit Breaker pattern?

The Circuit Breaker wraps a remote call and tracks its failure rate. When failures cross the threshold, it flips to OPEN and fails fast, then HALF_OPEN tests recovery.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class ProtectedAccountClient {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(ProtectedAccountClient.class.getName());

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "accountService", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Remote call to account-service
        LOG.info("Debit completed for " + accountId);
    }

    public void fallback(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, Throwable t) {
        LOG.warning("Circuit OPEN for accountService. Fallback for " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
State transitions:
CLOSED -> failure rate > threshold -> OPEN (fail fast)
OPEN -> wait duration expires -> HALF_OPEN (permit trial calls)
HALF_OPEN -> trial calls succeed -> CLOSED
```

### What is the Saga pattern?

The Saga pattern replaces two-phase commit. A payment saga debits then credits, and on failure runs a compensating action.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class TransferSaga {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(TransferSaga.class.getName());
    private final AccountClient accountClient;

    public TransferSaga(AccountClient accountClient) {
        this.accountClient = accountClient;
    }

    public void execute(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            accountClient.debit(fromId, amount);  // Step 1
            accountClient.credit(toId, amount);   // Step 2
            LOG.info("Saga completed: " + fromId + " -> " + toId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            accountClient.credit(fromId, amount);  // Compensation
            LOG.warning("Saga compensated: re-credited " + fromId);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (failure case):
WARNING: Saga compensated: re-credited SAV2001
```

### What is Event Sourcing?

Event Sourcing stores every change as an immutable event. Instead of updating a balance column, you append `AccountDebited` and `AccountCredited` events and rebuild the balance by replaying them.

```java
package com.bank.eventsourcing;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class EventSourcedAccount {

    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;
    private final List<DomainEvent> uncommittedEvents = new ArrayList<>();

    public EventSourcedAccount(String accountId) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
    }

    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds in " + accountId);
        }
        apply(new AccountDebited(accountId, amount, Instant.now()));
    }

    private void apply(DomainEvent event) {
        if (event instanceof AccountDebited d) {
            balance = balance.subtract(d.amount());
        } else if (event instanceof AccountCredited c) {
            balance = balance.add(c.amount());
        }
        uncommittedEvents.add(event);
    }

    // Replay from event store to reconstruct state
    public static EventSourcedAccount replayFrom(String accountId, List<DomainEvent> history) {
        EventSourcedAccount account = new EventSourcedAccount(accountId);
        history.forEach(account::apply);
        account.uncommittedEvents.clear();
        return account;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected event store:
1. AccountCredited(SAV2001, 100000.00, 2024-01-01T10:00:00Z)
2. AccountDebited(SAV2001, 50000.00, 2024-01-02T14:30:00Z)
3. AccountCredited(SAV2001, 25000.00, 2024-01-03T09:15:00Z)
Replay -> balance = 100000 - 50000 + 25000 = 75000.00
```

### What is CQRS?

CQRS separates writes from reads. The command side appends events while a read model is projected for fast queries.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

// Read-side projection: updates a materialized balance view
@Component
public class BalanceProjection {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(BalanceProjection.class.getName());
    private final BalanceViewRepository viewRepo;

    public BalanceProjection(BalanceViewRepository viewRepo) {
        this.viewRepo = viewRepo;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "account-events", groupId = "balance-projection")
    public void onEvent(AccountEvent event) {
        viewRepo.updateBalance(event.getAccountId(), event.getNewBalance());
        LOG.info("Balance view updated for " + event.getAccountId());
    }
}
```

```text
Command side: append events to event store
Read side: project into balance_view table for fast SELECT queries
Write scaling != Read scaling (optimize independently)
```

### What is the Strangler Fig pattern?

The Strangler Fig migrates a monolith gradually. The gateway routes some paths to a new service while the rest still hit the monolith, and you move one capability at a time until the monolith is gone.

```text
Phase 1: All traffic goes to monolith
  /api/v1/payments/** -> monolith
  /api/v1/accounts/** -> monolith

Phase 2: Extract Payment Service
  /api/v1/payments/** -> payment-service (new)
  /api/v1/accounts/** -> monolith

Phase 3: Extract Account Service
  /api/v1/payments/** -> payment-service
  /api/v1/accounts/** -> account-service (new)

Phase 4: Monolith deleted
```

### What are the Bulkhead and Sidecar patterns?

The Bulkhead pattern isolates resource pools so one failure cannot sink everything. The Sidecar pattern deploys a helper container beside each service for logging, proxying, or TLS.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class BulkheadAccountClient {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(BulkheadAccountClient.class.getName());

    @Bulkhead(name = "accountService", fallbackMethod = "bulkheadFallback")
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        LOG.info("Debit call within bulkhead for " + accountId);
    }

    public void bulkheadFallback(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, Throwable t) {
        LOG.warning("Bulkhead full for accountService. Rejecting call for " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Bulkhead: max 10 concurrent calls to account-service
  -> 11th call rejected immediately, does not consume thread pool
Sidecar (Istio/Envoy): runs as co-located container
  -> handles mTLS, observability, retries outside application code
```

### How do you choose which pattern to use?

Use the smallest pattern that solves your problem. Event Sourcing and CQRS add real complexity and are justified only when you need full audit history or very different read and write scaling.

| Pattern | Problem Solved | When to Use |
|---|---|---|
| API Gateway | Centralize routing, auth, rate limiting | Always in production |
| Service Discovery | Dynamic instance addresses | Always with multiple instances |
| Circuit Breaker | Cascading failures | Every remote call |
| Saga | Distributed transactions | Cross-service business operations |
| Event Sourcing | Audit trail, replay, temporal queries | Regulatory audit, financial ledgers |
| CQRS | Different read/write scaling | Heavy read workloads, complex queries |
| Strangler Fig | Monolith migration | Incremental modernization |
| Database-per-Service | Data coupling | Always; foundation of microservices |
| Bulkhead | Resource isolation | When one dependency can starve others |
| Sidecar | Cross-cutting infra concerns | Service mesh (Istio, Linkerd) |
